ctly to NOSE and EARS (inspection), HEIGHT (measurement), and additional exam elements including auscultation, skeletal system examination, complete skin exam, and checking for breast/nipple bleeding. These direct ties indicate that the community is less about multiple peer entities and more about standardized clinical workflow elements coordinated under the physical exam. [Data: Relationships (282, 281, 277, 11546, 11548,+more)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Head and neck inspection is represented through NOSE and EARS as routine physical-exam targets\",\"explanation\":\"NOSE is characterized as a routinely examined body area during a physical exam, typically via visual inspection and looking inside the nostrils to assess nasal passages. The network explicitly encodes this as a PHYSICAL EXAM → NOSE relationship describing examination of the nose during the exam. [Data: Entities (267); Relationships (282)]\\n\\nEARS are similarly described as anatomical structures inspected during a routine head and neck assessment, and the network includes a PHYSICAL EXAM → EARS relationship specifying that clinicians look in the ears as part of the physical exam. Together, these entities indicate an emphasis on head/neck screening as a standard component of general evaluation. [Data: Entities (266); Relationships (281)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Baseline body measurements include HEIGHT (and weight measurement is represented in relationships)\",\"explanation\":\"HEIGHT is explicitly defined as a body measurement taken and recorded during a physical exam, and the network includes a PHYSICAL EXAM → HEIGHT relationship stating it is commonly performed as part of a baseline assessment. This highlights a standardization aspect of the community: objective measurements are captured as part of routine care. [Data: Entities (262); Relationships (277)]\\n\\nThe relationships also include PHYSICAL EXAM → WEIGHT, describing that weight may be checked or measured during the exam and used for baseline and nutrition-related context. While the WEIGHT entity record is not included in the provided entities list, the relationship still indicates that weight measurement is treated as a routine component alongside height within the exam process. [Data: Relationships (276)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Auscultation is a defined technique within the physical exam focused on heart and lung assessment\",\"explanation\":\"AUSCULTATION is defined as listening with diagnostic intent to internal body sounds during a physical exam, most commonly focusing on lungs and heart. The network connects PHYSICAL EXAM to AUSCULTATION as an explicit component of the exam process, indicating cardiopulmonary evaluation as part of routine assessment. [Data: Entities (5221); Relationships (11546)]\\n\\nThis positioning within the community reflects how the physical exam combines observational/measurement components (e.g., height, ear/nose inspection) with technique-driven evaluation (auscultation) to assess organ system function. [Data: Entities (82, 5221); Relationships (11546, 277, 281, 282)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Screening for abnormal breast findings is represented via checking for breast/nipple bleeding\",\"explanation\":\"BREAST BLEEDING is described as bleeding from the nipple/breast region and is noted as something checked for during a breast examination as a potential abnormal finding. The network ties this directly to PHYSICAL EXAM instructions (PHYSICAL EXAM → BREAST BLEEDING), indicating that breast-focused checks may be incorporated into the broader exam when clinically indicated. [Data: Entities (3724); Relationships (7641)]\\n\\nThis element underscores that the physical exam community includes both general systems review steps and targeted screening for specific abnormal signs, depending on context and risk. [Data: Entities (82, 3724); Relationships (7641)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Musculoskeletal assessment and full-skin review appear as optional but enumerated physical-exam components\",\"explanation\":\"SKEL